<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lurs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lurs are an Iranian people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, living mainly in Lorestan, Kohgiluyeh and Boyer-Ahmad and neighbouring provinces. They speak Luri, a language closely related to Persian, and were long organised in pastoral tribes, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiari">Bakhtiari</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> Lur dynasties ruled Greater and Lesser Luristan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lurs.jpg" alt="Lurs" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shadegan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LuristanWomen.17Aug2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Perhaps a quarter or more of the population were pastoral nomads organised in tribes such as the Bakhtiari, Qashqai, Kurds, <b>Lurs</b> and Turkmen, who were also an important source of soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Zand army was a Zagros tribal force built around the cavalry and infantry of the Zand and allied Lak, <b>Lur</b> and other tribes, supplemented by Persian musketeers (tofangchi) and artillery that gave it a combined-arms capability greater than that of most of its Iranian rivals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bakhtiari are <b>Lur</b> tribes who migrate between winter pastures in Khuzestan and summer pastures in the high Zagros.</blockquote><a href="/state/bakhtiari-confederation">Bakhtiari confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty's founder, Abu Tahir ibn Muhammad, was a Kurdish or <b>Lur</b> commander in the service of the Salghurid dynasty of Fars.</blockquote><a href="/state/hazaraspid-dynasty">Hazaraspid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Shuja al-Din Khurshid, a <b>Lur</b> chief, took control of Lur-i Kuchik (Lesser Luristan), the mountains north of the Karkheh, around 1184, as the Seljuk Sultanate of Iraq declined.</blockquote><a href="/state/khorshidi-dynasty">Khorshidi dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
